# Notices and Attributions

This toolkit synthesizes ideas, framework shapes, and specific threshold values
from several publicly available sources. Each contribution is credited below.
Nothing in this repository was copied verbatim from unlicensed sources.

## MIT-licensed upstream inspiration

- **Fairness metric definitions and severity tiers** — adapted from the
  `validating-ai-ethics-and-fairness` skill in
  https://github.com/jeremylongshore/claude-code-plugins-plus-skills
  (MIT License, Jeremy Longshore). The four-fifths-rule classification
  (low 0.90–1.0, medium 0.80–0.90, high 0.70–0.80, critical <0.70) and the
  proxy-variable correlation threshold (r > 0.3) follow his skill's spec.

- **NIST AI RMF 1.0 and ISO/IEC 42001:2023 mapping patterns** — adapted from
  the `nist-ai-rmf` and `iso-42001-ai-governance` skills in
  https://github.com/mastepanoski/claude-skills (MIT License, Mario
  Stepanoski). Our checklist data files are re-derived from the public NIST
  and ISO source material but follow the mapping patterns these skills
  established.

- **Advisory framing and the six-principle vocabulary** — inspired by the
  `ai-ethics-advisor` agent in
  https://github.com/davila7/claude-code-templates (MIT License, Daniel
  Avila).

## Independently re-derived, not copied

- **Decision-making frameworks** (Five Questions, Five-Layer Impact Analysis,
  Consequence Scanning) — these are general ethical-review patterns derivable
  from public sources. Consequence scanning in particular originates in Agile
  product development and is described publicly by Doteveryone / the
  Consequence Scanning Manual (CC BY-SA). Our versions are written
  independently for this toolkit.

- **NIST AI RMF and ISO 42001 clause lists** — re-derived from the public
  primary documents, not from any third party's phrasing.

